		<tr>
			<td>
			<p style="line-height: 150%">I worked at Brigham Young University
			from 2003-2020. The first 12 years at BYU were great, with wonderful
			college deans like Van Gessel and John Rosenberg. I had lots of
			success (and was given also every research award available at BYU),
			and the &quot;BYU corpora&quot; were going great.</p>
			<p style="line-height: 150%">But then Scott Miller was appointed
			dean in 2015 and (for reasons that are still not clear to me), he
			went after me and the corpora, and this resulted in my eventual
			&quot;retirement&quot; in 2020. In the space of just four short years, the
			following events happened:</p>
			<ul>
				<li>
				<p style="line-height: 150%">In 2016-2017, Scott carried out an
				extensive 10-month <b>audit</b> of me and the corpora, against
				the advice of former college deans and department chairs. BYU
				eventually decided that there were no merits to his accusations.</li>
				<li>
				<p style="line-height: 150%">Scott threatened to <b>prevent me
				from having any access to the corpora</b> if I were to ever left
				BYU (which meant that the corpora would die).</li>
				<li>
				<p style="line-height: 150%">Once I decided to take the corpora
				off-campus (to protect the corpora from him), Scott <b>
				threatened</b> (out of spite) <b>to fire me from BYU</b> if I
				didn't continue publishing at an agreed-to level. This was in
				spite of the fact that I had been in the top 1-2% of all faculty
				in the college in terms of publishing during the previous 10-15
				years (while I developed the still on-campus corpora), and in
				spite of the fact that I had received virtually every research
				award given at BYU.</li>
				<li>Again, out of spite, Scott <b>refused</b> (for three weeks)
				<b>to</b> sign the standard BYU licensing agreement that would
				allow me to <b>take the corpora off-campus</b>. He only gave in
				once several other administrators on campus convinced him that
				this was the fair and reasonable thing to do.</li>
				<li>Within 10-15 minutes of signing the agreement, he <b>&quot;took&quot;
				more than $10,000</b> from academic licenses for the corpora and
				moved them to an account where I wouldn't have access to them
				(even though this money was from other universities, and was
				earmarked for corpus development).</li>
				<li>Once I confronted Scott Miller (and Jared Christenson, the
				college controller) about the missing $10,000, they sought to <b>
				conceal what they had done</b>, and finally claimed that it had
				all been a &quot;bad mistake&quot;, in spite of his clear emails ordering
				the transfer of the money.</li>
				<li>Finally, (again out of spite), Scott told my department that
				<b>I couldn't receive any more university money for any research</b>
				related to the corpora. Because virtually all of my research is
				related to the corpora, this meant that I would never receive
				any more support for research from BYU. Scott knew that this
				would be the final straw, which would <b>force me to leave BYU</b>.</li>
			</ul>
			<p>If someone (anyone) in BYU central administration had helped me
			with this situation, things might have been resolved, and I (and the
			corpora) would still be at BYU. But no one helped -- in spite of
			constant pleading on my part (thanks for nothing, Brad Neiger and
			David Paul and Shane Reese).</p>
			<p>So in a nutshell, that's what has happened. There is very good
			evidence (emails and/or witnesses) for all of the points discussed
			above; it's not just the ravings of a &quot;crazy prima donna&quot; professor.
			The fact that I was willing to retire at age 57 shows how bad things
			had become -- even though it meant that I would have to leave what
			had been my &quot;dream job&quot; and even though it meant that I would lose
			out on hundreds of thousands of dollars.</p>
			<p>Thanks, Scott Miller -- mission accomplished!</p>
			<p>&nbsp;</p>
			<p>
&nbsp;</td>
